   "t": "Денис, ты просто бессовестный племянник!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Denis, you are simply a shameless nephew!' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'Denis' and finishing on 'nephew'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "Приехал в Москву и молчал.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You came to Moscow and kept quiet.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'came' before landing on 'to be silent'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "Даже не позвонил любимой тёте!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You did not even call your beloved aunt!' Eleonora brings out the contrast between 'even' and 'not' in a few short words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "Как же я сильно соскучилась!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'How very much I have missed you!' Notice how Russian puts 'how' and 'missed' together in a short, direct phrase where word endings show how the ideas connect.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "denis",
   "t": "Я тоже очень соскучился, тётя.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I missed you very much too, Aunt.' Notice how Russian expresses 'I' and 'aunt' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "Он был здесь, но уехал.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'He was here, but he left.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'he' before landing on 'left'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "Кажется, он сегодня немного приболел.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'It seems he fell slightly ill today.' Notice how Russian expresses 'it seems' and 'fell slightly ill' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Eleonora answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
